WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.487
File creation is a moment of exposure.

2
00:00:02.567 --> 00:00:04.181
When a C program creates a file,

3
00:00:04.261 --> 00:00:08.359
it establishes an object that other users,
services, backup tools, scanners,

4
00:00:08.439 --> 00:00:10.053
or operators may be able to see.

5
00:00:10.133 --> 00:00:14.541
The permissions chosen at creation should
match the sensitivity of the data from the

6
00:00:14.621 --> 00:00:15.242
beginning.

7
00:00:15.322 --> 00:00:19.606
Creating a broad-access file and then
trying to restrict it afterward is a fragile

8
00:00:19.686 --> 00:00:20.183
pattern.

9
00:00:20.263 --> 00:00:22.498
Even if the later change usually succeeds,

10
00:00:22.578 --> 00:00:26.614
there can be a period where the file
exists with broader access than intended.

11
00:00:26.694 --> 00:00:30.047
If the permission change fails and the
program misses the error,

12
00:00:30.127 --> 00:00:31.617
the file may remain exposed.

13
00:00:31.697 --> 00:00:35.981
Prefer creation mechanisms that apply
restrictive access as part of the creation

14
00:00:36.061 --> 00:00:38.482
step where the platform supports that
design.

15
00:00:38.562 --> 00:00:42.846
On POSIX-style systems, process defaults
and umask behavior can affect the final

16
00:00:42.926 --> 00:00:43.671
permissions.

17
00:00:43.751 --> 00:00:46.918
Inherited directory settings, deployment
configuration,

18
00:00:46.998 --> 00:00:49.047
and file system policy may also matter.

19
00:00:49.127 --> 00:00:53.597
The implementation should not rely on
vague assumptions about defaults when the file

20
00:00:53.677 --> 00:00:55.043
contains sensitive data.

21
00:00:55.123 --> 00:00:58.165
Sensitive files are not limited to obvious
secret stores.

22
00:00:58.245 --> 00:01:01.846
Temporary output, logs, reports, exports,
caches, debug captures,

23
00:01:01.926 --> 00:01:04.969
and conversion products may include
identifiers, paths,

24
00:01:05.049 --> 00:01:08.588
configuration values, customer content, or
operational details.

25
00:01:08.668 --> 00:01:10.530
If the program creates those files,

26
00:01:10.610 --> 00:01:13.839
it should classify them and choose
permissions accordingly.

27
00:01:13.919 --> 00:01:17.458
Creation should combine clear access
intent with checked results.

28
00:01:17.538 --> 00:01:19.959
If a file was supposed to be created
privately,

29
00:01:20.039 --> 00:01:24.386
the code should use the platform's
appropriate creation options and verify errors

30
00:01:24.466 --> 00:01:27.446
from creation and any permission-sensitive
follow-up.

31
00:01:27.526 --> 00:01:31.437
A failure to create the file safely should
be treated as a real failure of the

32
00:01:31.517 --> 00:01:33.380
workflow, not an ignorable detail.
